Chapter: IdentityManager
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256
  cites Foreword chapter Ed25519
  cites Foreword chapter Aes256
  cites Foreword chapter Hkdf
  cites UI chapter KeyInput
  cites Kernel chapter DiskFacts

 Identity lifecycle manager. Orchestrates the first-boot ceremony
 (generate keypair, encrypt with passphrase, store to DiskFacts),
 the unlock flow (prompt passphrase, derive key, decrypt, verify,
 load to pinned kernel memory), lock and timeout.

 The private key never exists on disk in plaintext. At runtime it
 lives in a pinned kernel memory region accessible only via the
 key-load (syscall 15), key-zero (syscall 16), and key-status
 (syscall 18) intrinsics, which carry the Identity effect and are
 refused by the kernel for any process whose capability word lacks
 the identity bit.

 To idm-first-boot runs the first-boot identity ceremony.
 To idm-unlock prompts for passphrase and loads the key.
 To idm-lock zeros the pinned key.
 To idm-is-unlocked checks if a key is loaded.
 To idm-check-timeout locks if the timeout has elapsed.
 To idm-change-passphrase re-encrypts with a new passphrase.
 To idm-copy-to-drive copies the encrypted identity to another DiskFactStore.

 We say:

Section: Constants

  idm-lock-timeout-ticks : Integer = 32400
  idm-hkdf-info : List Integer = [99, 111, 100, 101, 120, 45, 105, 100, 101, 110, 116, 105, 116, 121]

Section: Types

  IdmResult =
    | IdmOk
    | IdmWrongPassphrase
    | IdmNoIdentity
    | IdmError (Text)

  IdmOutcome = record {
    idm-store : DiskFactStore,
    idm-result : IdmResult
  }

Section: First-Boot Ceremony

  idm-first-boot : DiskFactStore, Integer -> [Console, Identity] IdmOutcome
  idm-first-boot (store) (timestamp) = act
    let seed-bytes = idm-read-device-seed
    in act
      kb-entropy <- idm-collect-keyboard-entropy
      let combined = sha256 (seed-bytes & kb-entropy)
      in let seed = idm-hash-words-to-bytes combined
      in let keypair = idm-generate-keypair seed
      in let pub-key = keypair.ikp-public
      in let priv-key = keypair.ikp-private
      in let fingerprint = idm-fingerprint pub-key
      in act
        idm-show-lines [
          "",
          "  Identity created.",
          "  Fingerprint: " & fingerprint,
          ""
        ]
        passphrase <- idm-prompt-passphrase-create
        when passphrase
         is None -> IdmOutcome { idm-store = store, idm-result = IdmError "Passphrase mismatch" }
         is Just (pp-bytes) ->
        let salt = idm-read-random-bytes 16
        in let iv = idm-read-random-bytes 16
        in let derived-bytes = idm-derive-key salt pp-bytes
        in let encrypted = aes256-cbc-encrypt (aes256-pkcs7-pad priv-key) derived-bytes iv
        in let rec = IdentityRecord {
          id-version = 1,
          id-salt = salt,
          id-iv = iv,
          id-encrypted-key = encrypted,
          id-public-key = pub-key,
          id-created-ticks = timestamp
        }
        in let store2 = sysdb-write-identity store rec timestamp
        in let default-config = SysBootConfig {
          sbc-boot-mode = sysdb-boot-mode-console,
          sbc-boot-partition = 0,
          sbc-boot-drive-serial = ""
        }
        in let store3 = sysdb-write-boot-config store2 default-config timestamp
        in let store4 = disk-checkpoint store3
        in act
          loaded <- idm-load-key-to-pinned priv-key
          let zeroed = idm-zero-list pp-bytes 0
          in let zeroed2 = idm-zero-list derived-bytes 0
          in let zeroed3 = idm-zero-list priv-key 0
          in act
            idm-show-lines [
              "  Identity saved and loaded.",
              "  You are now authenticated.",
              ""
            ]
            IdmOutcome { idm-store = store4, idm-result = IdmOk }
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end

Section: Unlock Flow

  idm-unlock : DiskFactStore -> [Console, Identity] IdmOutcome
  idm-unlock (store) =
    let id-maybe = sysdb-find-identity store
    in when id-maybe
      is None -> IdmOutcome { idm-store = store, idm-result = IdmNoIdentity }
      is Just (rec) -> act
        pp-bytes <- idm-prompt-passphrase "  Passphrase: "
        let derived-bytes = idm-derive-key (rec.id-salt) pp-bytes
        in let decrypted-padded = aes256-cbc-decrypt (rec.id-encrypted-key) derived-bytes (rec.id-iv)
        in let decrypted = aes256-pkcs7-unpad decrypted-padded
        in let verify-pub = ed25519-public-key decrypted
        in if idm-bytes-eq verify-pub (rec.id-public-key) then idm-unlock-accept store pp-bytes derived-bytes decrypted
        else idm-unlock-refuse store pp-bytes derived-bytes decrypted
      end

 The two outcomes are named functions rather than branches written inline,
 because the accepting one has to load the key into pinned memory before it
 wipes the plaintext, and that load is effectful: inline it needs an act-bind
 nested inside an if inside an act, which is the exact shape that has scoped
 an `in` to the wrong `let` before now. A single-line dispatch to two named
 functions cannot do that. Both branches zero every plaintext buffer on every
 path -- the refusing one zeroes the same three the accepting one does, which
 is the property worth reading them side by side to check.

  idm-unlock-accept : DiskFactStore, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> [Identity] IdmOutcome
  idm-unlock-accept (store) (pp-bytes) (derived-bytes) (decrypted) = act
    loaded <- idm-load-key-to-pinned decrypted
    let zeroed = idm-zero-list pp-bytes 0
    in let zeroed2 = idm-zero-list derived-bytes 0
    in let zeroed3 = idm-zero-list decrypted 0
    in IdmOutcome { idm-store = store, idm-result = IdmOk }
  end

  idm-unlock-refuse : DiskFactStore, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> IdmOutcome
  idm-unlock-refuse (store) (pp-bytes) (derived-bytes) (decrypted) =
    let zeroed = idm-zero-list pp-bytes 0
    in let zeroed2 = idm-zero-list derived-bytes 0
    in let zeroed3 = idm-zero-list decrypted 0
    in IdmOutcome { idm-store = store, idm-result = IdmWrongPassphrase }

Section: Lock

 These three reach the pinned key through key-zero and key-status, so they
 carry [Identity] and say so. The row is not bookkeeping: Identity is a real
 capability with a manifest bit, and a program that can lock, unlock, or ask
 after the private key is exercising authority a program that merely prints
 does not have. Declaring it here is what makes that authority visible in the
 signature of everything above -- up to and including first-boot's own entry
 point, which is the one place a reader should be able to see it.

  idm-lock : [Identity] Integer
  idm-lock = key-zero

  idm-is-unlocked : [Identity] Boolean
  idm-is-unlocked = key-status == 1

  idm-check-timeout : Integer -> [Identity] Integer
  idm-check-timeout (current-tick) =
    if key-status == 0 then 0
    else let lock-tick = idm-read-lock-tick
    in if lock-tick == 0 then idm-set-lock-tick current-tick
    else if current-tick - lock-tick > idm-lock-timeout-ticks then key-zero
    else 0

  idm-reset-timeout : Integer -> Integer
  idm-reset-timeout (current-tick) = idm-set-lock-tick current-tick

Section: Passphrase Change

  idm-change-passphrase : DiskFactStore, Integer -> [Console] IdmOutcome
  idm-change-passphrase (store) (timestamp) =
    let id-maybe = sysdb-find-identity store
    in when id-maybe
      is None -> IdmOutcome { idm-store = store, idm-result = IdmNoIdentity }
      is Just (rec) -> act
        old-pp <- idm-prompt-passphrase "  Current passphrase: "
        let old-derived-bytes = idm-derive-key (rec.id-salt) old-pp
        in let decrypted-padded = aes256-cbc-decrypt (rec.id-encrypted-key) old-derived-bytes (rec.id-iv)
        in let decrypted = aes256-pkcs7-unpad decrypted-padded
        in let verify-pub = ed25519-public-key decrypted
        in if idm-bytes-eq verify-pub (rec.id-public-key) == False then
          let z0 = idm-zero-list old-pp 0
          in let z1 = idm-zero-list old-derived-bytes 0
          in let z2 = idm-zero-list decrypted 0
          in IdmOutcome { idm-store = store, idm-result = IdmWrongPassphrase }
        else act
          new-pp <- idm-prompt-passphrase-create
          when new-pp
          is None ->
            let z0 = idm-zero-list old-pp 0
            in let z1 = idm-zero-list old-derived-bytes 0
            in let z2 = idm-zero-list decrypted 0
            in IdmOutcome { idm-store = store, idm-result = IdmError "Passphrase mismatch" }
          is Just (new-pp-bytes) ->
            let new-salt = idm-read-random-bytes 16
            in let new-iv = idm-read-random-bytes 16
            in let new-derived-bytes = idm-derive-key new-salt new-pp-bytes
            in let new-encrypted = aes256-cbc-encrypt (aes256-pkcs7-pad decrypted) new-derived-bytes new-iv
            in let new-rec = IdentityRecord {
              id-version = 1,
              id-salt = new-salt,
              id-iv = new-iv,
              id-encrypted-key = new-encrypted,
              id-public-key = rec.id-public-key,
              id-created-ticks = rec.id-created-ticks
            }
            in let store2 = sysdb-write-identity store new-rec timestamp
            in let store3 = disk-checkpoint store2
            in let z0 = idm-zero-list old-pp 0
            in let z1 = idm-zero-list old-derived-bytes 0
            in let z2 = idm-zero-list decrypted 0
            in let z3 = idm-zero-list new-pp-bytes 0
            in let z4 = idm-zero-list new-derived-bytes 0
            in IdmOutcome { idm-store = store3, idm-result = IdmOk }
        end
      end

Section: Install Copy

  idm-copy-to-drive : DiskFactStore, DiskFactStore, Integer -> DiskFactStore
  idm-copy-to-drive (source-store) (target-store) (timestamp) =
    let id-maybe = sysdb-find-identity source-store
    in when id-maybe
      is Just (rec) -> let store2 = sysdb-write-identity target-store rec timestamp
        in disk-checkpoint store2
      is None -> target-store

Section: Kernel Memory Interface

  idm-load-key-to-pinned : List Integer -> [Identity] Integer
  idm-load-key-to-pinned (key-bytes) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 32
    in let w = idm-poke-list buf key-bytes 0
    in key-load buf

  idm-poke-list : Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  idm-poke-list (buf) (bs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length bs then 0
    else if i >= 32 then 0
    else let w = poke-byte buf i (list-at bs i)
    in idm-poke-list buf bs (i + 1)

  idm-read-lock-tick : Integer
  idm-read-lock-tick =
    let buf-addr = 30648
    in peek-byte buf-addr 0 + bit-shl (peek-byte buf-addr 1) 8
       + bit-shl (peek-byte buf-addr 2) 16 + bit-shl (peek-byte buf-addr 3) 24

  idm-set-lock-tick : Integer -> Integer
  idm-set-lock-tick (tick) =
    let buf-addr = 30648
    in let w0 = poke-byte buf-addr 0 (bit-and tick 255)
    in let w1 = poke-byte buf-addr 1 (bit-and (bit-shru tick 8) 255)
    in let w2 = poke-byte buf-addr 2 (bit-and (bit-shru tick 16) 255)
    in poke-byte buf-addr 3 (bit-and (bit-shru tick 24) 255)

Section: Entropy Collection

  idm-read-device-seed : List Integer
  idm-read-device-seed = idm-peek-bytes 30576 32 0 []

  idm-peek-bytes : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  idm-peek-bytes (addr) (len) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else idm-peek-bytes addr len (i + 1) (list-push acc (peek-byte addr i))

  idm-read-random-bytes : Integer -> List Integer
  idm-read-random-bytes (count) =
    let seed-addr = 30576
    in idm-peek-bytes seed-addr count 0 []

  idm-collect-keyboard-entropy : [Console] List Integer
  idm-collect-keyboard-entropy = act
    idm-show-lines [
      "",
      "  Type a random sentence for additional entropy,",
      "  then press Enter:",
      "  "
    ]
    let input = idm-read-line-bytes
    in let ticks-after = get-ticks
    in input & [bit-and ticks-after 255, bit-and (bit-shru ticks-after 8) 255]
  end

Section: Keypair Generation

  IdmKeypair = record {
    ikp-private : List Integer,
    ikp-public : List Integer
  }

  idm-generate-keypair : List Integer -> IdmKeypair
  idm-generate-keypair (seed-words) =
    let seed-bytes = idm-take-bytes seed-words 32
    in let pub = ed25519-public-key seed-bytes
    in IdmKeypair { ikp-private = seed-bytes, ikp-public = pub }

  idm-take-bytes : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  idm-take-bytes (words) (n) = idm-take-loop words n 0 []

 Take n bytes, padding a short source with zeros. The padding branch used
 to push one zero and return, so idm-take-bytes [7,7] 4 answered [7,7,0]:
 three bytes, not four, and no further padding. It recurses now.

 It has never mattered, and that is not a reason to leave it: the only
 caller is idm-generate-keypair, whose seed is idm-hash-words-to-bytes of
 a sha256 -- eight words, exactly 32 bytes -- so the short path was never
 taken. A helper that pads on request must pad, or the first caller with
 a shorter source gets a key built from a truncated seed and no error.

  idm-take-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  idm-take-loop (src) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if i >= list-length src then idm-take-loop src n (i + 1) (list-push acc 0)
    else idm-take-loop src n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at src i))

Section: Fingerprint

  idm-fingerprint : List Integer -> Text
  idm-fingerprint (pub-key) =
    let hash = sha256 pub-key
    in sha256-to-hex hash

Section: Console I/O Helpers

 `idm-rlb-loop` reads through `poll-key`, not `uefi-read-key`. The raw
 call returns a PS/2 Set-1 make code, and this loop tests its result
 against ASCII: Enter's make code is 28, never 13, so a loop over the raw
 call cannot terminate and every byte it collects is a scancode. That is
 the bug the skip reason "blocks waiting for keyboard input" was
 describing.

 `poll-key` has no shift latch yet, so a letter arrives as its UPPERCASE
 ASCII code whatever the typist did. A passphrase is therefore folded to
 uppercase before it is ever hashed. This is a property of the stored
 identity, not of this loop: teaching `poll-key` to decode shift would
 change what these bytes are, and every passphrase created before that day
 would stop unlocking its key. Fold here deliberately, or migrate, but do
 not let it change by accident.

  idm-show-lines : List Text -> [Console] Integer
  idm-show-lines (lines) = idm-show-loop lines 0 (list-length lines)

  idm-show-loop : List Text, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Integer
  idm-show-loop (lines) (i) (len) =
    if i >= len then 0
    else act
      print-line-uni (list-at lines i)
      idm-show-loop lines (i + 1) len
    end

  idm-prompt-passphrase : Text -> [Console] List Integer
  idm-prompt-passphrase (prompt) = act
    print-uni prompt
    idm-read-line-bytes
  end

  idm-prompt-passphrase-create : [Console] Maybe (List Integer)
  idm-prompt-passphrase-create = act
    print-uni "  Create a passphrase: "
    let pp1 = idm-read-line-bytes
    in act
      print-uni "  Confirm passphrase:  "
      let pp2 = idm-read-line-bytes
      in if idm-bytes-eq pp1 pp2 then
        let z = idm-zero-list pp2 0
        in Just pp1
      else
        let z0 = idm-zero-list pp1 0
        in let z1 = idm-zero-list pp2 0
        in act
          print-line-uni "  Passphrases do not match."
          None
        end
    end
  end

  idm-read-line-bytes : List Integer
  idm-read-line-bytes = idm-rlb-loop []

  idm-rlb-loop : List Integer -> List Integer
  idm-rlb-loop (acc) =
    let ch = poll-key
    in if ch == 0 then idm-rlb-loop acc
    else if ch == 13 then acc
    else if ch == 10 then acc
    else idm-rlb-loop (list-push acc ch)

Section: Byte Utilities

  idm-bytes-eq : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  idm-bytes-eq (a) (b) =
    if list-length a /= list-length b then False
    else idm-bytes-eq-loop a b 0 (list-length a) 0

  idm-bytes-eq-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  idm-bytes-eq-loop (a) (b) (i) (len) (diff) =
    if i >= len then diff == 0
    else idm-bytes-eq-loop a b (i + 1) len (bit-or diff (bit-xor (list-at a i) (list-at b i)))

  idm-zero-list : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  idm-zero-list (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then 0
    else let w = list-set-at xs i 0
    in idm-zero-list xs (i + 1)

 The passphrase-derived AES-256 key. It was written out at all four sites
 that need it -- both encrypts and both decrypts -- and it was wrong at
 all four, in the same way: each ran hkdf's result through
 idm-hash-words-to-bytes.

 hkdf returns BYTES. hkdf-expand converts every hmac block with
 hkdf-words-to-bytes and then takes `length` of them, so `hkdf ... 32` is
 32 bytes, and codex/test/apps/hkdf-vector-test pins exactly that against
 RFC 5869. Expanding them again turned each byte b into [0,0,0,b]: a
 128-element key, 97 of them zero, of which AES-256 takes the first 32 --
 eight bytes of real entropy in a 256-bit key.

 Nothing could see it. The same wrong key encrypted and decrypted, so the
 ceremony round-tripped: a passphrase set at first boot unlocked
 correctly, and the only symptom was a number nobody read. That is why it
 is one function now -- a derivation written four times is a derivation
 that can be wrong four times -- and why idm-key-tests asserts its LENGTH
 rather than round-tripping it. A round trip agrees with itself.

 idm-hash-words-to-bytes stays: sha256 really does return words, and the
 first-boot seed above is the one place that is the right conversion.

  idm-derive-key : List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  idm-derive-key (salt) (pp-bytes) = hkdf salt pp-bytes idm-hkdf-info 32

  idm-hash-words-to-bytes : List Integer -> List Integer
  idm-hash-words-to-bytes (words) = idm-hw2b words 0 (list-length words) []

  idm-hw2b : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  idm-hw2b (words) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let w = list-at words i
    in idm-hw2b words (i + 1) len (acc & [bit-and (bit-shru w 24) 255, bit-and (bit-shru w 16) 255, bit-and (bit-shru w 8) 255, bit-and w 255])

